(#5332)
[CA]
Consideration of FAA Grant #3-56-0005-43 between the City of Cheyenne, the County
of Laramie, and the Cheyenne Regional Airport Board, in the amount of $324,621.00, for an
update to the Airport’s Master Plan.  Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried.  (#5333)
Consideration of a Joint Powers Board Agreement between the County of Laramie, the
City of Cheyenne, and Laramie County Community College, to create the Laramie County Early
Education and Development Joint Powers Board.  Reporting for Finance Committee, Mr. Valdez
moved to approve, seconded by Ms. Broyles.  Concerns were expressed regarding government
competition with private business, and city involvement in the program. Ken Dugas, Early
Education Partnership Board member, explained the purpose of the Joint Powers Board (JPB)
proposal, stating the early education facility to be constructed would serve approximately 185
students, and reviewed anticipated building, personnel and operating costs. Discussion included
the following topics: Cost of facility construction ($4,000,000.00) and operating costs; fiscal
concerns relative to future funding requests to the City; proposed application by the JPB for a
Wyoming Business Council grant in the amount of $3,000,000.00 for the facility; lack of child
care facilities locally and state-wide; support for the Early Education Partnership; Laramie
County Community College involvement; community needs, and budgetary concerns. Upon
inquiry, Mr. Dugas provided information on the proposed budget plan and operating budget;
purpose of the JPB, which is to assist with financing for facility construction, and future
possibility of leasing the facility to an operator.  John Fafoutakis expressed concerns with
government competing with the private sector. Further comments followed on facility
```

### Page 5 of 7

```text
construction costs and identification of the funding source to cover the difference between the
cost of construction vs. grant funds, if approved, from the Wyoming Business Council. Rod
Hottle, United Way, provided information on monetary donations that have been received and/or
pledged toward the Early Education facility. Upon further inquiry, Mr. Dugas explained the
Partnership Board’s decision to seek formation of a JPB and discussed quality early care and
education; aspects involving student admission criteria and enrollment costs; seeking possible
scholarships to help off-set costs to facility users and potential business/employee cost sharing;
flexible facility hours; quality of educational staff to be hired; program similarity to the
Montessori School, and attendance costs compared to other community facilities. Upon inquiry,
Connie Sloan-Cathcart, Director of Laramie County United Way, advised they have been a
partner in the Early Education Partnership program from its inception and that the agency
supports the Partnership’s efforts. Concern was expressed with certain provisions contained in
the agreement relative to an “opt out” clause for JPB members, and dissolution of the JPB. Upon
inquiry, Tim Thorson, Laramie County Grants Manager, reviewed the process that has been
followed for drafting and seeking required approvals for the JPB agreement, stating it was
modeled after an agreement utilized in Sheridan, and indicated the agreement meets the
requirements of state statutes pursuant to the JPB Act.  Upon inquiry concerning insurance
coverage, Erik Rasmussen, Deputy City Attorney, confirmed the JPB would not be a covered
Board through the Wyoming Association of Risk Management. Mr. Hottle and Ms. Sloan-
Cathcart indicated once the JPB agreement is approved, insurance would be in place prior to the
effective starting date of the JPB.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body with the exception of Ms. Ash, Mr. Collins and Dr. Rinne voting “no”.  (#5334)
Lease Agreement between the City of Cheyenne and L and L, Inc., for the use of the
Kingham Prairie View Clubhouse and the provision of golf services at the Kingham Prairie View
Golf Course.  Mike LePore, L & L, Inc. requested a 45 day postponement to allow time to
provide further information.  Mr. Valdez reported there was no recommendation on the item
from Finance Committee.  Mr. Pierson moved to approve, seconded by Mr. Spiker.  Mr. Pierson
moved to postpone to March 23, 2009, seconded by Ms. Broyles.  It was advised the
postponement will provide time to meet with L and L, Inc. to discuss financial matters involving
services provided for the city’s golf courses.  Motion to postpone carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body.
Professional Services Agreement between the City of Cheyenne and L and L, Inc., for the
provision of golf services at the Airport Golf Course.  Mike LePore, L & L, Inc., requested a 45
day postponement to allow time to provide further information.  Mr. Valdez reported there was
no recommendation on the item from Finance Committee. Mr. Pierson moved to approve,
seconded by Mr. Spiker.  Mr. Pierson moved to postpone to March 23, 2009, seconded by Mr.
Spiker.  Motion to postpone carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body.
